\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two scalar frames for a permutation orbit}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Let #{S_3} permute the three standard basis vectors of
#{\mathbb R^3}. Choose #{\phi^\circ=e_1}, with stabilizer
#{G^\circ=\{1,(23)\}}. A section sends #{e_1,e_2,e_3} to
#{1,(12),(13)}. On an edge from scalar #{e_1} to scalar #{e_2}
with link #{(12)}, the [reduced link](fdip-010X) is the identity.
Change only the frame at #{e_2} to #{(12)(23)}, composing
permutations right to left. The reduced link becomes #{(23)}.
These different elements represent the same residual orbit.}

\p{The single-link double cosets are #{G^\circ} and
#{G^\circ(12)G^\circ}. Multiplying #{(12)} independently on the
left and right by #{1} or #{(23)} gives the #{4} permutations
moving #{1}. Hence for positive real hopping the two factors are
#{\ue^{w_e}} on the stabilizer and #{1} off it, since the two
different basis vectors are orthogonal. For any graph the
counting factor is #{3^{|X|}}.}

\p{The count of two double cosets specializes
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.card_doubleCosetQuotient_stabilizer}
to the permutations of three points.}
